---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle =
  "Unit 5: Hash Functions and Hash Tables";
const unit = "Unit 5";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  unit={unit}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
    {pageTitle}
  </h1>
  <hr class="mb-6" />

  <!-- Collision Resolution (Expanded & Applied) -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Collision Resolution Techniques
      (Detailed)
    </summary>

    <p>
      <b>Collision:</b> Two keys map to the
      same index: h(k1) = h(k2). CRTs are
      applied to resolve collisions at the
      table index level.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      1. Open Addressing
    </h4>
    <p>
      All data stays inside the array.
      If a slot is full, probe for
      another.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        <b>Linear Probing</b>: index =
        (hash_code + i) % m;
        cache-friendly; weak: primary
        clustering.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Quadratic Probing</b>: index
        = hash_code + c1·i + c2·i²;
        reduces primary clustering;
        weak: secondary clustering.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Double Hashing</b>: index =
        hash_code + i·hash2(key);
        minimal clustering; more
        computation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      2. Separate Chaining
    </h4>
    <p>
      Each slot stores multiple
      elements. CRT manages the chain,
      not the index.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>Linked list</li>
      <li>List heads</li>
      <li>
        BST or dynamic array for long
        chains
      </li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      3. Coalesced Hashing
    </h4>
    <p>
      Hybrid of chaining + open
      addressing. Colliding items are
      linked inside the array.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      4. Robin Hood Hashing
    </h4>
    <p>
      During insert, the key with longer
      probe distance swaps with the new
      key to equalize probe lengths.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      5. Cuckoo Hashing
    </h4>
    <p>
      Two hash functions → two possible
      locations. If occupied, displace
      existing key. Lookup is O(1)
      worst-case.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      6. Hopscotch Hashing
    </h4>
    <p>
      Maintains a small neighborhood
      around each bucket using bitmaps.
      Very cache-friendly and
      concurrency-friendly.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      7. Dynamic Resizing
    </h4>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        Full resize: Allocate larger
        table, rehash all elements.
      </li>
      <li>
        Incremental resize: Gradually
        move elements to avoid long
        pauses.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-2">
      <b>Key Rule:</b> Keep load factor α
      = n / m controlled for performance.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Implementations -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Hash Table Implementations in C
    </summary>

    <!-- Linear Probing -->
    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      1. Open Addressing – Linear
      Probing
    </h4>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code class="language-c">
{`
#include <stdio.h>

#define TABLE_SIZE 10
#define EMPTY -1

int table[TABLE_SIZE];

int hash(int key) {
    return key % TABLE_SIZE;
}

void init() {
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++)
        table[i] = EMPTY;
}

void insert(int key) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++) {
        int probe = (idx + i) % TABLE_SIZE;
        if(table[probe] == EMPTY) {
            table[probe] = key;
            return;
        }
    }
}

int search(int key) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++) {
        int probe = (idx + i) % TABLE_SIZE;
        if(table[probe] == EMPTY) return 0;
        if(table[probe] == key) return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
`}
</code></pre>

    <!-- Separate Chaining -->
    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      2. Separate Chaining
    </h4>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code class="language-c">
{`
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define TABLE_SIZE 10

typedef struct Node {
    int key;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* table[TABLE_SIZE];

int hash(int key) {
    return key % TABLE_SIZE;
}

void insert(int key) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->key = key;
    newNode->next = table[idx];
    table[idx] = newNode;
}

Node* search(int key) {
    int idx = hash(key);
    Node* curr = table[idx];
    while(curr) {
        if(curr->key == key) return curr;
        curr = curr->next;
    }
    return NULL;
}
`}
</code></pre>

    <!-- Double Hashing -->
    <h4 class="font-semibold mt-3">
      3. Open Addressing – Double
      Hashing
    </h4>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code class="language-c">
{`
#include <stdio.h>

#define TABLE_SIZE 10
#define EMPTY -1

int table[TABLE_SIZE];

int hash1(int key) { return key % TABLE_SIZE; }
int hash2(int key) { return 1 + (key % (TABLE_SIZE - 1)); }

void init() {
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++)
        table[i] = EMPTY;
}

void insert(int key) {
    int idx = hash1(key);
    int step = hash2(key);
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++) {
        int probe = (idx + i * step) % TABLE_SIZE;
        if(table[probe] == EMPTY) {
            table[probe] = key;
            return;
        }
    }
}

int search(int key) {
    int idx = hash1(key);
    int step = hash2(key);
    for(int i = 0; i < TABLE_SIZE; i++) {
        int probe = (idx + i * step) % TABLE_SIZE;
        if(table[probe] == EMPTY) return 0;
        if(table[probe] == key) return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
`}
</code></pre>
  </details>

  <!-- Performance Summary -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Performance Summary
    </summary>
    <ul class="list-disc ml-6 mt-2">
      <li>
        Separate chaining: Average O(1),
        worst O(n)
      </li>
      <li>
        Open addressing: O(1) if α low
      </li>
      <li>
        Cuckoo hashing: O(1) worst-case
        lookup
      </li>
      <li>
        Robin Hood: Lower variance in
        probe length
      </li>
      <li>
        High load factor → exponential
        collision growth
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-2">
      <b>Exam Insight:</b> Always relate performance
      to load factor (α).
    </p>
  </details>
</BaseLayout>
